{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-015",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "LINQ operators deferred execution IEnumerable IQueryable and async sequences",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "LINQ is a family of query patterns with a shared vocabulary for filtering, projection, ordering, grouping, joining, aggregation, and materialization. The same-looking call can execute locally, build a provider expression, or asynchronously consume a stream.",
    "Enumerable operators work with IEnumerable<T> and delegate values; many are deferred until enumeration, while terminal operators execute immediately. Re-enumerating a deferred query can repeat work or observe changed source data.",
    "Queryable operators work with IQueryable<T> and expression trees. A provider may translate only a subset of expression-tree forms; a query that cannot translate can fail, switch to client-side work at an explicit boundary, or have provider-specific behavior.",
    "AsyncEnumerable operators work with IAsyncEnumerable<T> and asynchronous delegates. Enumeration is asynchronous and cancellation is cooperative; async LINQ does not make CPU-bound work parallel or make a database provider translate arbitrary C#.",
    "Choose the execution boundary deliberately: keep filtering, ordering, joins, and paging on a remote provider until the intended materialization point, then use local operators over the result. Measure data volume, allocation, and provider queries rather than trusting syntax alone."
  ],
